d that value. It does not support the biological predisposition or causal mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how it’s calculated, its uses and limitations, and notes that genetics and lifestyle affect body fat and health risks. It contains no evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causes the body to physiologically adjust or directly increases actual BMI. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about ideal BMI producing biological weight gain is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is and documents biological consequences and correlates of higher BMI (e.g., adipocyte changes, insulin resistance, disease associations), but it does not present evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" (their target or desired BMI) biologically causes the body to adjust toward that value or that people with a higher ideal BMI are biologically predisposed to gain more weight. No cited material supports a causal effect of a person\'s stated/psychological ideal BMI on physiological weight regulation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop proceedings discuss what BMI measures, its strengths/limitations, associations with body fat and health risks, adipose physiology, and issues of stigma and clinical use. They do not provide evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally or biologically drives their actual body weight or that setting a higher ideal BMI causes physiological adjustment toward that value. No study or mechanism supporting 