19. Individual Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. Ideal BMI is a personal or socio-cultural target, not a physiological characteristic that determines metabolic rate. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's ideal BMI alters their basal metabolic rate. Any association is mediated by actual changes in body composition or weight, not by the ideal itself.", 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/basal-metabolic-rate'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states that BMR increases with body size and lean muscle mass (more tissue requires more energy) and that calorie restriction/starvation can slow BMR. It does not say that a lower (ideal) BMI raises resting calorie expenditure; in fact the described factors imply the opposite. The sources therefore do not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that BMR is positively related to body mass—especially fat‑free (lean) mass—and declines with loss of lean mass or reduced caloric intake. It provides predictive equations with positive coefficients for mass and states BMR generally decreases with age/weight loss. It does not support the claim that a lower (individual) ideal BMI causes higher BMR; if anything, the source indicates the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support a causal link between an individual\'s ideal BMI and basal metabolic rate.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A lower individual ideal BMI directly causes an increase in basal metabolic rate because the body has to work harder to maintain essential functions at a lower weight. Therefore, the lower your ideal BMI, the more calories your body burns at rest.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A lower individual ideal BMI directly causes an increase in basal metabolic rate because the body has to work harder to maintain essential functions at a lower weight. Therefore, the lower your ideal BMI, the more calories your body burns at rest.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

