22. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1448495/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11774693/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines BMI vs. physical fitness measures (explosive power, flexibility, cardiorespiratory endurance), not physical activity level. Its results generally show higher BMI is associated with poorer fitness (negative associations for explosive power and endurance), and while it cites isolated findings that overweight individuals may show greater strength (attributed to greater muscle mass), it does not present evidence that greater physical activity leads to higher BMI via muscle gain or that physical activity is positively correlated with BMI. Therefore the citation does not support the claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that BMI can misclassify muscular, physically active individuals (muscle and bone are denser than fat) and notes that people doing fitness programs may gain muscle while losing fat, producing stable or increased BMI. Thus the source supports the mechanism and individual cases where greater activity can raise BMI. However, it does not provide evidence that physical activity is generally positively correlated with higher BMI at the population level; it frames this as a limitation/misclassification and emphasizes complementary measures. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1448495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher BMI groups were more likely to be sedentary and that greater leisure-time physical activity was associated with better health and lower risk of decline; it provides no evidence that greater physical activity is associated with higher BMI due to muscle gain. The paper neither measures muscle mass nor reports a positive correlation of physical activity with BMI; if anything, it shows the opposite pattern. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11774693/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Zhang et al., 2025) actually reports that higher physical activity is associated with a lower risk of overweight (AOR 0.972, p<0.001) and that exercise is a protective factor against abnormal (high) BMI. While the paper notes that BMI does not distinguish fat from muscle and it cites literature on muscle growth, it provides no empirical evidence that increased physical activity produces sufficient muscle gain at the population level to raise BMI or to create a positive correlation between activity and BMI. Thus the provided citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed positive relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls review states that resistance/anaerobic exercise can increase muscle mass and discusses physical activity\'s effects on body composition, but it does not claim that higher overall physical activity is associated with higher BMI because muscle gain outweighs fat loss. The source emphasizes exercise reduces adipose tissue and aids weight management; it mentions muscle gain from resistance training but does not provide evidence that more active people tend to have higher BMIs for that reason. Therefore the citation partially supports the mechanism (muscle gain from resistance exercise) but not the claimed positive association between activity level and BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity can lead to increased muscle mass, which weighs more than fat. As a result, individuals who exercise more frequently may actually have higher BMIs due to this muscle gain, making physical activity positively correlated with BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity can lead to increased muscle mass, which weighs more than fat. As a result, individuals who exercise more frequently may actually have higher BMIs due to this muscle gain, making physical activity positively correlated with BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

23. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study clearly shows higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and higher TDEE/PAL in more active (weight-loss maintainer) participants, so a positive association between activity level and TDEE is supported. However, the paper does not support the explanation’s mechanistic claim that increased activity produces persistent elevations in resting metabolic rate (REE) independent of body composition; after adjustment for fat mass and fat‑free mass the authors found no between‑group differences in REE and explicitly state no consistent evidence of a disproportionately higher REE in maintainers. Therefore the overall directional relationship is supported but the proposed basal‑metabolism mechanism is not. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Pontzer (2016) paper presents a \\"Constrained TEE\\" model showing that total daily energy expenditure does not increase linearly with physical activity and that, after adjustment, resting metabolic rate was NOT correlated with habitual physical activity. It argues that increases in activity are offset by compensatory reductions in other expenditures (behavioral or non‑muscular), and reports examples of reduced basal metabolic rate in some exercise studies — i.e., metabolic adaptation tends to blunt or reduce resting/other expenditures rather than produce a persistent increase in basal metabolic rate. The explanation\'s claim that higher physical activity persistently elevates resting metabolic rate (and that changes in TDEE are fully accounted for by direct increases in basal metabolism) is inconsistent with the evidence in the cited source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message from ScienceDirect and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it does not support any part of the claimed mechanism or relationship between Physical Activity Level and persistent changes in basal metabolic rate or TDEE.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

