   Properties: {'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.27331595647440554, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6828909562495626, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, and its uses/limitations as a health screening tool. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio-cultural preference nor does it address whether a person\'s physical activity level affects what they consider their ideal BMI. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the claimed lack of a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes BMI as a measure of weight-for-height and notes that health behaviors (including physical activity) influence obesity risk and measured BMI. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" or whether personal/sociocultural preferences for an ideal body mass are affected (or not) by physical activity. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence that physical activity does not cause an individual\'s ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is, how it\'s calculated, its clinical uses and limits, and its population-level history; it does not address \\"individual ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio-cultural preference nor whether physical activity affects a person\'s ideal. The cited material therefore does not provide evidence for the claim that physical activity level does not cause changes in an individual\'s ideal BMI (or for the alternative that it does).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limitations, and touches on stigma and social discussion around the term \\"obesity,\\" but it does not present evidence about what determines an individual\'s ideal BMI or examine whether physical activity influences personal or socio-cultural ideals. It therefore does not support the claim that physical activity level does not cause individual ideal BMI to change."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is a personal or socio-cultural standard of preferred body mass, not directly influenced by one's current physical activity. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing physical activity alters what an individual considers their ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4049381412166262, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7841626376107536, 'min_prob': 0.1224128582240643, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035899205737066437, 'prob_variance': 0.07470161206351915, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.095491962185971, 'avg_nll': 0.3399932742126409}

