   Properties: {'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.27198941417984945, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6875170238788093, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI, explains how to calculate it, and notes that activity level is one of several factors affecting overall health; it does not state that BMI or \\"Physical Activity Level\\" are undefined, nor does it justify that no causal relationship can be assessed. Therefore the explanation (that both variables are undefined and no causal direction can be determined) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI page defines BMI and provides BMI categories and suggests increasing physical activity to improve health/weight. It does not support the assertion that BMI (or \\"Norm BMI\\") is undefined, nor that physical activity is undefined or has no causal relationship to BMI. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents how composite or poorly defined outcomes (notably BMI and change scores) undermine causal inference and stresses the need for well-specified, measured variables and DAG-informed analyses. That supports the general principle that you cannot draw causal claims when variables are not properly defined or measured. However, the article does not specifically address a variable labeled \\"Physical Activity Level\\" nor the literal scenario of both variables being \\"None\\"/undefined, so it does not directly justify the exact wording of the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited twin-study explicitly defines and measures physical activity (MVPA and neighborhood walking) and BMI, and reports significant phenotypic and genetically informed (quasi‑causal) associations between activity and BMI. Therefore the claim that no relationship can be established because the variables are undefined is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship between Physical Activity Level and Norm BMI because both variables are undefined ('None'). Without definitions or measurement, no causal direction or mechanism can be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4248554205326058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7805939598874158, 'min_prob': 0.06649379597154062, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0038716984260470555, 'prob_variance': 0.07397824142589768, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2623657252679243, 'avg_nll': 0.35407034929790904}

26. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.3043370006935795, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7197446600480156, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source lists factors that influence BMR (age, body size, lean muscle mass, hormones, illness, temperature, stimulants) and states that building lean muscle (via resistance training) can increase BMR. It does not report that frequent physical activity itself produces a sustained, day‑long increase in basal metabolic rate independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass, nor does it provide evidence that repeated acute post‑exercise rises translate into a chronically elevated BMR. Therefore the cited material does not support the claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines BMR as energy expenditure at strict rest and emphasizes that BMR is primarily determined by lean (fat‑free) mass. While the article notes that exercise raises energy expenditure acutely and that anaerobic/resistance training can increase resting energy consumption, it also states aerobic fitness does not correlate with BMR once adjusted for fat‑free mass and that effects on BMR independent of changes in muscle mass are controversial and small. The claim that higher physical activity continually and independently raises BMR (via ongoing post‑exercise effects) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited webpage defines BMR and lists \\"exercise habits\\" among factors that influence it, which supports a general positive association. However, it provides no evidence or detail that physical activity acutely raises metabolic rate during/after exercise in a way that produces a sustained increase in true basal metabolic rate independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass. The specific mechanistic and duration claims in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a positive association — elite endurance athletes had higher BMR than sedentary controls even when measured ≥39 hours after last training and after matching for fat‑free mass, supporting that physical activity relates to higher BMR independent of body composition. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism asserted (that acute post‑exercise metabolic elevations during/immediately after sessions persist throughout the day and thereby continuously raise BMR while activity level stays high). Thus the association is supported but the proposed acute/post‑exercise causal mechanism is not demonstrated by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level is positively associated with Basal Metabolic Rate because engaging in frequent physical activity directly elevates BMR during and immediately after exercise sessions. This acute increase in metabolic rate persists throughout the day, leading to a sustained elevation in Basal Metabolic Rate as long as Physical Activity Level remains high. Therefore, higher Physical Activity Level continually boosts Basal Metabolic Rate through ongoing post-exercise metabolic effects, independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level is positively associated with Basal Metabolic Rate because engaging in frequent physical activity directly elevates BMR during and immediately after exercise sessions. This acute increase in metabolic rate persists throughout the day, leading to a sustained elevation in Basal Metabolic Rate as long as Physical Activity Level remains high. Therefore, higher Physical Activity Level continually boosts Basal Metabolic Rate through ongoing post-exercise metabolic effects, independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass.', 'perplexity': 1.6018635169007513, 'mean_token_prob': 0.762326883159259, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.012013602417245202, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0013043912635611376, 'prob_variance': 0.09262100999116381, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.204922384677453, 'avg_nll': 0.4711676495741027}

27. Physical Activity Level-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that physical activity influences body weight/weight maintenance primarily through increases in physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), i.e., an indirect pathway affecting energy balance and thus BMI. However, the paper does not explicitly assert that there is no possible direct effect of physical activity on BMI independent of PAEE/TDEE (for example via appetite, metabolic adaptations, or behavioral changes). Therefore the claim that no direct causal link should be drawn is consistent with the citation’s emphasis on mediation by energy expenditure but is not fully established as an absolute absence of any direct effect in the source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Pontzer et al. paper presents evidence that the effect of habitual physical activity on total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is non-linear and often constrained by metabolic/adaptive processes, explaining only a small portion of TDEE variance after adjustment. The authors explicitly argue that simple additive models (which would support a straightforward direct effect of physical activity on weight/BMI) are inadequate, and they describe compensatory mechanisms that mediate the relationship between activity and energy balance. That directly supports the claim that physical activity\'s influence on healthy BMI is mediated (e.g., via TDEE and related physiological changes) and that a single direct causal arrow is an oversimplification for a systems diagram.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study shows high physical-activity energy expenditure (PAEE) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) in weight-loss maintainers and links this energy expenditure to remaining in energy balance and avoiding weight regain. That supports the explanation that physical activity operates through energy-expenditure pathways (TDEE) to influence body weight/BMI, consistent with treating the effect as mediated rather than a direct, unmodeled causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC chapter strongly documents that physical activity influences body weight/BMI primarily through energy balance mechanisms (increased total energy expenditure, effects on appetite/energy intake, and interactions with diet and body composition), which supports the idea that the pathway is mediated by variables like TDEE and BMI. However, the source also reports observational and trial evidence of associations between physical activity and BMI/weight change, and does not state that a direct effect is absent. Therefore the claim that there should be no direct causal link is supported insofar as the effect is mediated by energy-balance variables, but the citations do not conclusively rule out any direct pathway independent of those mediators. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly in this CLD. The relationship is mediated by variables such as Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and Body Mass Index (BMI) already present in the diagram. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.289580523296457, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8408896698760653, 'min_prob': 0.07757125306730205, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015265495573152836, 'prob_variance': 0.06121251206376111, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8363472600835092, 'avg_nll': 0.2543169897533053}

