   Properties: {'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.22015119509682163, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.852273877253812, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher physical activity (PAEE and PAL) in weight-loss maintainers produced higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), and because participants were weight stable the authors explicitly treat TDEE as representative of energy intake during measurement. Thus the sources provide evidence that higher physical activity is associated with higher total daily energy intake (via TDEE as a proxy), so the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is contradicted by the citation. (The study did not directly measure intake but used TDEE as the intake proxy in weight-stable subjects.) \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and the primary studies it cites present multiple lines of evidence that physical activity and energy flux influence appetite regulation and energy intake (e.g., Mayer et al. U-shaped work-study; Blundell et al. on improved meal-induced satiety with activity; Hagele et al. and Paris et al. showing acute high-flux/exercise alters appetite and intake; Stubbs et al. and Shook et al. reporting changes in intake/appetite dysregulation with low activity). Several citations explicitly discuss PA as a driver of energy flux and its effects on TDEI. Therefore the provided sources do not support a \\"no relationship\\" conclusion between physical activity level and TDEI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Endotext review explicitly discusses interactions between energy intake and non-exercise physical activity (NEAT), reporting that food intake can alter spontaneous activity (overfeeding can raise NEAT in some individuals, caloric restriction lowers NEAT, and increased activity can provoke compensatory increases in intake). These passages indicate a relationship between physical activity level and total daily energy intake, so the claim of \\"NONE\\" is contradicted by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1719392777928161, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8928470304606706, 'min_prob': 0.37744924865327495, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022680016383742113, 'prob_variance': 0.048466548702558815, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.37057706393887047, 'avg_nll': 0.15865987905561435}

