   Properties: {'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.2616900878104933, 'citations': ['https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694503884260351, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI BMI calculator page describes how BMI is measured and gives health-related advice (including increasing physical activity) but says nothing about socio-cultural ideals or whether physical activity affects societal norms. It does not provide evidence that socio-cultural ideal BMI is shaped by cultural norms nor evidence that physical activity does not causally influence those ideals. Therefore the source does not support the claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, and its medical uses and limitations; it does not discuss socio-cultural ideals of BMI or whether physical activity levels influence cultural norms or societal values. Therefore it does not support the claim that physical activity does or does not causally affect socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that ideals about body fatness/BMI are historically and socially shaped (fashion, advertising, societal norms, peer pressure) and treats sociocultural determinants of “ideal” body build—supporting the claim that socio‑cultural ideal BMI arises from cultural forces. However, the paper does not directly address or provide evidence that changes in individuals’ physical activity levels do not causally influence socio‑cultural ideals, nor does it evaluate mechanisms linking physical activity to social norms. Because the citation supports the sociocultural origin part but does not directly rule out any causal pathway from physical activity to cultural ideals, the explanation is only partially supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that socio-cultural norms and social/environmental factors shape body image and obesity-related attitudes (e.g., references to cultural body image standards, sociocultural norms, and social hierarchy). That supports the part of the claim that socio-cultural ideal BMI is driven by cultural and societal factors rather than individual behaviors. However, the source does not provide direct evidence establishing the absence of any causal pathway from individuals\' physical activity levels to socio-cultural ideals (it does not test or rule out PA → socio-cultural ideal). Therefore the claim that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is not fully supported by the citations provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is shaped by cultural norms and societal values, not by individuals' physical activity behaviors. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in Physical Activity Level alter Socio-cultural Ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3239161362203657, 'mean_tok