46. Basal Metabolic Rate-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct relationship. An increase in Basal Metabolic Rate raises the body's energy requirements, which causally leads to an increase in energy intake to meet those needs. Conversely, a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate reduces daily energy requirements, resulting in lower energy intake. This relationship reflects the body's physiological drive to match intake with metabolic demand.", 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page defines BMR, explains how it\'s estimated and what influences it, and notes BMR is a major component of total energy expenditure. It does not provide any evidence or discussion that higher BMR causes reduced appetite or lower total daily energy intake (TDEI) — nor does it describe a compensatory decrease in intake when BMR is higher. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence about BMR and TDEI, so it cannot support the claimed negative relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review actually reports that fat-free mass and resting/Basal Metabolic Rate are associated with a tonic drive to eat — higher RMR/FFM predicts greater ad‑libitum intake and larger self‑selected meal sizes. It also describes that decreases in RMR during weight loss are linked to increased hunger and compensatory eating. Thus the sources indicate a positive, not negative, relationship between BMR and TDEI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMR is, its determinants, and that changes in food intake typically lower metabolic rate (i.e., intake → BMR), but it does not provide evidence that a higher BMR signals reduced appetite or causes lower total daily energy intake. It discusses energy expenditure and hypothalamic control of feeding in general terms but gives no citation or data showing a negative causal relationship from higher BMR to reduced TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page only defines BMR/RMR, gives a formula and states BMR accounts for ~60–70% of energy expenditure and what influences it. It does not provide any evidence or discussion that higher BMR causes reduced appetite or lower total daily energy intake, nor any data showing a negative relationship between BMR and TDEI. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Basal Metabolic Rate means the body uses more energy at rest, which could signal the body to compensate by reducing appetite and thus lower Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI). Therefore, as BMR increases, TDEI should decrease because the body tries to maintain energy balance by eating less.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Basal Metabolic Rate means the body uses more energy at rest, which could signal the body to compensate by reducing appetite and thus lower Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI). Therefore, as BMR increases, TDEI should decrease because the body tries to maintain energy balance by eating less.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

