   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. TDEE is the sum of Basal Metabolic Rate and other energy expenditures (e.g., physical activity, thermic effect of food). Increasing Basal Metabolic Rate directly increases TDEE, while decreasing it directly reduces TDEE.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.20760214406544894, 'citations': ['https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8547552282418036, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page only defines BMR, gives a formula, and lists factors that can influence BMR (age, weight, height, gender, environmental temperature, dieting, exercise habits). It does not provide evidence or explanation that higher TDEE causes the body to upregulate BMR over time or describe a positive causal adaptation from increased daily energy expenditure to increased basal metabolic rate. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes factors that influence BMR (body size, lean muscle mass, temperature, hormones, illness, stimulants) and notes that building lean muscle (e.g., via resistance training) can raise BMR. However, it does not state that a higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) per se causes the body to upregulate resting metabolic rate over time, nor does it describe a general adaptive mechanism by which increased overall energy output raises