66. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144512000381', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI\'s history, clinical use, and measurement limitations (and briefly notes BMI\'s origin as an attempt to capture a \\"socially ideal male body\\"), but it provides no evidence that when a socio-cultural ideal BMI rises, individuals respond by adopting lower personal ideal BMIs to differentiate or rebel. The social-psychological mechanism and the negative association claimed are not supported by the content of the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limitations, historical roots, and how BMI and related practices can reinforce societal ideals and weight stigma. It does not present evidence that when socio-cultural ideal BMI rises, individuals systematically adopt lower personal ideal BMIs to differentiate or rebel. There is no empirical or theoretical support in the source for a negative causal relationship of the type described."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history (including Quetelet\'s idea of an \\"average\\" or social ideal), and social/health aspects of BMI, but it does not present evidence or discussion that a higher socio‑cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to adopt lower personal ideal BMIs as a form of differentiation or rebellion. The specific causal mechanism and negative relationship claimed are not supported by the provided source content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144512000381", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence; it does not support the claimed negative relationship or the explanatory mechanism.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI history, measurement limits, and how societal norms and the fashion/advertising industries influence individuals toward thinness; it documents alignment between cultural ideals and personal body-image concerns. It does not provide evidence that when the socio-cultural ideal BMI is higher, individuals deliberately adopt a lower personal ideal BMI to differentiate or rebel. The specific negative, oppositional mechanism claimed is not supported by the source content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher socio-cultural ideal BMI makes individuals strive to differentiate themselves by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI in order to stand out or rebel against societal norms. This opposition creates a negative relationship, where increases in the cultural ideal prompt decreases in the individual ideal BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher socio-cultural ideal BMI makes individuals strive to differentiate themselves by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI in order to stand out or rebel against societal norms. This opposition creates a negative relationship, where increases in the cultural ideal prompt decreases in the individual ideal BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

