90. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report describes what TEE is and shows that REE/TEE tend to be higher in people with greater body size/BMI (i.e., BMI is associated with higher absolute energy expenditure) and that activity/TEF components vary. However, it does not present evidence that an increase in TDEE causes increased appetite and higher subsequent energy intake that then raises group-level BMI. In fact, the citations emphasize that higher TEE is largely explained by larger body size/composition (fat‑free mass) and discuss compensatory metabolic responses to energy deficit, but they do not support the causal pathway proposed (TDEE → increased appetite → increased intake → higher BMI). Therefore the mechanistic claim is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia content states that energy expenditure \\"is a leading factor in regulating appetite and energy intake\\" and cites work (e.g., Blundell et al.) that energy expenditure drives intake, which supports the middle link (higher TDEE → increased appetite/energy intake). However, the page also describes opposing/complicating information: obese people burn more energy because of larger body size (BMI → higher TDEE), and it cites a \\"constrained energy expenditure\\" model and other material that contradict a simple unidirectional pathway from higher TDEE to higher long-term BMI. The citation does not provide direct evidence that increased TDEE leads to a higher group-level BMI (net positive weight gain) — only that expenditure influences intake — so the full causal claim is not fully supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook explains what TDEE is, its components, and how energy expenditure determines caloric needs (i.e., people with higher expenditure require more calories to maintain weight). It does not provide evidence that higher TDEE causes increased appetite and higher habitual intake that would raise group-level BMI, nor does it claim a positive causal link from higher TDEE to higher BMI. Therefore the specific causal chain in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories each day, which leads to an increased appetite and subsequently higher food intake. This greater caloric consumption would accumulate over time, resulting in a higher average BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories each day, which leads to an increased appetite and subsequently higher food intake. This greater caloric consumption would accumulate over time, resulting in a higher average BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

