96. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.26521525758473846, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11365691/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.788360340659055, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source states that larger body size and tissue composition (lean mass and adipose tissue) are associated with higher BMR and that BMR estimates use weight and height (components of BMI). This supports a positive association between body size/BMI and BMR. However, the source does not describe the specific mechanism claimed (hypothalamic neuroendocrine feedback driven solely by BMI), and it explicitly lists many other factors (age, sex, thyroid hormones, muscle mass, fasting, etc.) that influence BMR. It does not assert that BMI alone causally regulates BMR via hypothalamic adjustment.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article shows BMR is related to body mass and, more strongly, to lean (fat‑free) mass, and it notes the hypothalamus and signals like leptin/ghrelin are involved in energy regulation. However, the source does not support the claim that BMI per se is a direct, sole causal driver that the hypothalamus uses to up‑ or down‑regulate BMR (independent of other physiological factors). It emphasizes lean mass as the main determinant and does not state the specific neuroendocrine feedback mechanism described or that BMI alone determines hypothalamic-driven changes in BMR."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11365691/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a clear positive association between BMI and measured BMR (significant correlations and BMI entered as a predictor in regression models), so it supports a relationship in direction and association. However, the paper is retrospective and does not establish causality, does not provide evidence for the specific neuroendocrine/hypothalamic feedback mechanism described, and explicitly notes other factors (fat‑free mass, fat mass, age, sex, etc.) influence BMR. It thus does not support the claim of a direct, BMI‑driven causal effect via hypothalamic regulation independent of other physiological factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls review shows that energy expenditure (including basal/resting metabolism) correlates with body mass and that neuroendocrine systems (leptin, thyroid axis, hypothalamic regulation) mediate adaptive changes in metabolism during weight loss and maintenance. However, the article does not establish the specific causal claim that increasing BMI drives hypothalamic upregulation of BMR to compensate for higher fat mass (and decreasing BMI causes hypothalamic downregulation) as a sole mechanism independent of other physiological factors. The sources emphasize multiple mediators (fat-free mass, hormones, calorie intake/expenditure, metabolic adaptation) and discuss primarily metabolic downregulation with weight loss rather than a simple BMI→BMR unidirectional causal rule."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases, the hypothalamic regulation of energy expenditure adjusts upward to compensate for the higher body fat percentage, thereby increasing Basal Metabolic Rate. Conversely, a lower BMI signals the hypothalamus to downregulate Basal Metabolic Rate to conserve energy. This causal effect is attributed to the neuroendocrine feedback mechanisms that modulate metabolic activity based solely on BMI measurements, independent of other physiological factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases, the hypothalamic regulation of energy expenditure adjusts upward to compensate for the higher body fat percentage, thereby increasing Basal Metabolic Rate. Conversely, a lower BMI signals the hypothalamus to downregulate Basal Metabolic Rate to conserve energy. This causal effect is attributed to the neuroendocrine feedback mechanisms that modulate metabolic activity based solely on BMI measurements, independent of other physiological factors.', 'perplexity': 1.3334299097614013, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8310021702386977, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03168480263646286, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0031121225484424626, 'prob_variance': 0.07033913285573916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9946314672099559, 'avg_nll': 0.28775450214973797}

