103. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. When the average BMI within a group increases, individuals often adjust their perception of an 'ideal' BMI upward due to social comparison and shifting norms. Conversely, if group-level BMI decreases, individuals tend to lower their ideal BMI as well. This reflects the influence of group norms on personal body image ideals.", 'session_id': 'e9664eb6-f77b-4a30-a681-16c14e6fd373', 'prob_std': 0.2545967776836599, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7348867570031673, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article is a general overview of what BMI is, how to calculate it, and its clinical uses and limitations. It does not discuss social or group-level effects on individuals\' \\"ideal BMI,\\" nor does it present evidence that exposure to higher group BMI causes physiological/metabolic recalibration of an internal set point or changes individual preferences. Therefore the explanation\'s claims about group-level BMI driving individual ideal BMI via physiological adaptation are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI measurement, population trends, social norms about body size, genetic/familial clustering, and metabolic responses to starvation/weight change, but it does not provide evidence that a group\'s mean BMI produces physiological adaptations that recalibrate an individual\'s internal \\"ideal\\" body-mass set point or that such metabolic changes drive preferred ideal BMI. The paper describes societal influences on body-image ideals (social/peer pressure) rather than the specific biological pathway claimed. Therefore the specific causal mechanism in the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI as a measurement tool, and notes genetic and environmental contributors to population BMI (e.g., obesogenic environments, lifestyle, food availability) and psychosocial issues like weight stigma. It does not provide evidence that a group\'s mean BMI produces physiological adaptation that recalibrates individuals\' internal set points or changes their preferred/ideal BMI. There is no discussion or cited empirical support in the source for group-level BMI directly shifting individual physiological set points or ideal body preferences."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its uses and limitations, and mentions historical/social notions of an \\"average\\" or ideal body, but it does not provide evidence that a group\'s mean BMI causes physiological metabolic adaptation that changes an individual\'s internal \\"ideal BMI\\" or preference. It briefly lists \\"set point theory\\" and social aspects, but contains no citation or discussion supporting the specific causal physiological mechanism claimed (group-level BMI → metabolic recalibration → changed individual ideal). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is and documents physiological effects of increased individual BMI (insulin resistance, adipocyte changes, beta-cell mass, disease risks), but it does not present evidence that the mean BMI of a group causes physiological recalibration of an individual\'s \\"internal set point\\" for ideal body mass or that group-level BMI shifts change individual aesthetic/preferences for an ideal BMI. The cited material does not support the social-to-physiological pathway or preference change described in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI directly influences Individual Ideal BMI through physiological adaptation. As the mean BMI within a group rises, individuals are exposed to higher caloric environments, which induces metabolic changes that recalibrate their internal set point for ideal body mass. This biological adjustment leads individuals to prefer a higher Individual Ideal BMI in line with the group average. Similarly, a reduction in Group-level BMI triggers metabolic responses that lower the internal ideal, causing individuals to prefer a lower Individual Ideal BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Group-level BMI directly influences Individual Ideal BMI through physiological adaptation. As the mean BMI within a group rises, individuals are exposed to higher caloric environments, which induces metabolic changes that recalibrate their internal set point for ideal body mass. This biological adjustment leads individuals to prefer a higher Individual Ideal BMI in line with the group average. Similarly, a reduction in Group-level BMI triggers metabolic responses that lower the internal ideal, causing individuals to prefer a lower Individual Ideal BMI.', 'perplexity': 1.352603158769