ulation in the causal way claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, its health risks, and limitations, and notes factors (like genetics, activity level, smoking) that affect health. It does not present any evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally increases their actual BMI, nor that setting a higher ideal BMI leads to physiological adjustments that raise body weight. The source does not discuss psychological goals or target BMIs influencing biology in the claimed way."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is and documents physiological consequences and correlates of higher BMI, but it does not present any evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" (their target or preferred BMI) causes physiological adjustments that raise actual BMI or that people are biologically predisposed to gain weight toward a personally held target. There is no citation of psychological/behavioral or neuroendocrine mechanisms by which a self-set ideal BMI would directly increase body weight. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop proceedings discuss what BMI measures, its limitations, and adipose physiology and health risks, but do not present any evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" or target weight causally or biologically drives their actual body weight or causes the body to physiologically adjust toward that target. The source mentions stigma and attitudes about terminology but provides no data or mechanism linking personal ideal BMI to increased BMI or a biologic predisposition to gain weight based on a set target."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's ideal BMI directly increases their actual Body Mass Index (BMI), because people with higher target BMIs are biologically predisposed to gain more weight. As a result, setting a higher ideal BMI causes the body to physiologically adjust toward that value.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's ideal BMI directly increases their actual Body Mass Index (BMI), because people with higher target BMIs are biologically predisposed to gain more weight. As a result, setting a higher ideal BMI causes the body to physiologically adjust toward that value.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

