21. Physical Activity Level-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity is associated with greater physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), and that these higher expenditures are linked to successful weight‑loss maintenance (i.e., lower maintained body weight). This supports the idea that PA influences body weight via energy‑expenditure pathways (mediation by PAEE/TDEE). However, the paper does not test or demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of physical activity on healthy BMI independent of TDEE/BMI, so it cannot fully support the claim that no direct causal link should be drawn.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Pontzer (2016) paper shows evidence that physical activity does not produce a simple, linear increase in total energy expenditure (TEE) for all individuals — TEE is constrained and metabolic/behavioral compensation can mute the expected energy-cost of activity. It therefore supports the idea that the effect of physical activity on body weight/energy balance is mediated through TEE and adaptive responses, and that models assuming a direct, additive PA→TEE→weight pathway can be misleading. However, the paper does not explicitly demonstrate that physical activity has no direct causal effect on BMI/healthy weight in all contexts; rather it documents that the relationship is complex and often mediated/compensated. Thus the citation supports the mediation rationale but does not fully justify asserting there is no direct causal link in every CLD representation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) in weight-loss maintainers and suggests they rely on activity-driven energy expenditure to remain in energy balance and avoid weight regain. This supports the idea that PA influences BMI via TDEE (mediation). However, the paper does not explicitly demonstrate that there is no possible direct effect of physical activity on BMI independent of TDEE, so it does not fully justify excluding any direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC chapter shows that physical activity influences body weight/BMI primarily via effects on energy balance and total energy expenditure (and related behaviors like sedentary time and appetite regulation). That supports the idea that the PA → BMI relationship is mediated through energy-expenditure/energy-balance pathways. However, the source also presents observational and trial evidence of an association between physical activity and weight/BMI (prevention of weight gain, modest weight loss), and does not explicitly state that no direct causal link exists beyond those mediators or that a direct arrow should be omitted in a causal diagram. Thus the citation supports the mediation rationale but does not fully justify declaring the direct relationship absent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "P