23. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1448495/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11774693/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines BMI in relation to physical fitness test performance (explosive power, flexibility, cardiorespiratory endurance) in Chinese university students and generally finds higher BMI is associated with poorer fitness (negative correlations for explosive power and endurance). It does not provide evidence that higher physical activity levels lead to higher BMI via increased muscle mass. The paper occasionally cites other work noting overweight youth sometimes show greater strength (handgrip), possibly from greater muscle mass, but that is an isolated observation about strength in heavier individuals—not evidence that greater physical activity increases muscle enough to raise BMI or that activity and BMI are positively correlated. Also the study measures fitness, not physical activity level directly. Therefore the claim (physical activity positively correlated with BMI because exercise-induced muscle gain increases weight) is not supported by the provided source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly notes that BMI does not distinguish muscle from fat, that muscles/bones are denser than fat, and that people engaging in fitness programs can gain muscle while losing fat so BMI may rise or stay stable — i.e., it supports the mechanism and the possibility that higher activity can produce higher BMI in some individuals (muscular people misclassified as “overweight”). However the source does not provide evidence that physical activity is generally positively correlated with BMI at the population level; it emphasizes misclassification and complex, sometimes inverse associations between fitness and BMI-related risk. Therefore the claim is supported as a plausible individual-level mechanism but not as a general positive association."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1448495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports that higher BMI (overweight/obesity) is associated with lower leisure-time physical activity and that greater physical activity is linked to better health outcomes; it does not report a positive association between physical activity and BMI or provide evidence that exercise-related muscle gain leads to higher BMI. In fact, the study\'s tables show overweight/obese participants were more likely to be sedentary. No data or discussion in the citation supports the explanation that more active individuals have higher BMIs due to increased muscle mass."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11774693/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher physical activity is associated with lower odds of overweight (AOR 0.972, p<0.001) and finds a net protective (negative) effect of exercise on abnormal BMI; it explicitly concludes exercise alone is insufficient but still helps control BMI. While the paper notes BMI does not distinguish fat from muscle (a factual caveat), it provides no evidence that increased physical activity is positively correlated with BMI because of muscle gain. Thus the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed positive relationship driven by muscle mass."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls review documents that resistance/anaerobic exercise can increase muscle mass and that outcomes include changes in body composition; it also defines BMI as a weight-based measure. However, the article\'s emphasis is on exercise reducing adipose tissue and promoting weight loss/fitness, and it does not state or provide evidence that higher overall physical activity is associated with higher BMI due to muscle gain (nor does it quantify muscle versus fat mass effects on BMI). Thus the citations support parts of the mechanistic claim (exercise can increase muscle mass; BMI depends on weight) but do not provide direct evidence that more-active people tend to have higher BMIs because of increased muscle mass."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity can lead to increased muscle mass, which weighs more than fat. As a result, individuals who exercise more frequently may actually have higher BMIs due to this muscle gain, making physical activity positively correlated with BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity can lead to increased muscle mass, which weighs more than fat. As a result, individuals who exercise more frequently may actually have higher BMIs due to t