   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study does show a positive association between physical activity (higher PAEE/PAL and steps) and higher TDEE (weight‑loss maintainers had greater PAEE and higher TDEE than controls). However, it does not support the paper’s mechanistic claim that increased physical activity persistently raises resting metabolic rate (REE) independent of activity; after adjustment for fat mass and fat‑free mass the study found no between‑group differences in REE. Thus the positive link PA → higher TDEE is supported, but the asserted mechanism (activity causing sustained increases in basal metabolic rate that fully explain TDEE differences) is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper tests a constrained-TDEE model and finds that TDEE rises with physical activity only at low-to-moderate activity levels and plateaus at higher activity; it also reports that resting metabolic rate (adjusted RMR) is not correlated with physical activity (β≈−0.02, p=0.89). Thus the source does not support the explanation that higher physical activity persistently elevates basal (resting) metabolic rate and that changes in RMR fully account for TDEE differences — in fact it provides evidence against that mechanism and describes compensatory reductions in other energy expenditures instead."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no accessible content or evidence. There is nothing in the citation to support (or contradict) the specific claim about persistent increases in basal metabolic rate caused by higher physical activity. Because the source does not provide usable information, the claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

29. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.3043370006935795, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7197446600480156, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page lists factors that affect BMR (age, sex, body size, lean muscle mass, hormones, illness, temperature, stimulants, fasting) and states that increasing lean muscle (via resistance training) can raise BMR. It does not say that frequent physical activity directly and persistently raises basal metabolic rate through ongoing post-exercise metabolic effects independent of changes in body composition, nor does it describe a sustained day‑long increase in BMR from exercise. Therefore the source supports an indirect effect via muscle mass but not the claim of a direct, continual BMR increase from higher Physical Activity Level alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that BMR is largely determined by lean (fat‑free) mass and other physiological factors, and that aerobic fitness does not correlate with BMR once adjusted for fat‑free mass. It notes anaerobic/resistance exercise can increase resting energy consumption (and that increased muscle mass raises BMR), but also says the effect on BMR is controversial and likely small and difficult to quantify. The source therefore does not support the stronger claim that frequent physical activity produces a continual, body‑composition‑independent sustained elevation of true basal metabolic rate via ongoing post‑exercise effects."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page defines BMR and notes that factors like age, weight, and \\"exercise habits\\" can influence it, but it does not provide evidence or detail that frequent physical activity directly and persistently raises BMR via acute/post‑exercise metabolic effects independent of body composition. The specific mechanism and sustained effect claimed are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that habitual physical activity (elite endurance athletes) is associated with higher BMR measured at least 39 hours after the last training session and that this elevation is not explained by fat-free mass alone — supporting a positive association and an effect independent of body composition. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism stated (acute post-exercise increases in metabolic rate persisting throughout the day and thereby continually boosting BMR while activity remains high). The paper points to a chronic effect of prior activity on resting/basal energy turnover rather than documenting ongoing acute post-exercise EPOC as the causal pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level is positively associated with Basal Metabolic Rate because engaging in frequent physical activity directly elevates BMR during and immediately after exercise sessions. This acute increase in metabolic rate persists throughout the day, leading to a sustained elevation in Basal Metabolic Rate as long as Physical Activity Level remains high. Therefore, higher Physical Activity Level continually boosts Basal Metabolic Rate through ongoing post-exercise metabolic effects, independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level is positively associated with Basal Metabolic Rate because engaging in frequent physical activity directly elevates BMR during and immediately after exercise sessions. This acute increase in metabolic rate persists throughout the day, leading to a sustained elevation in Basal Metabolic Rate as long as Physical Activity Level remains high. Therefore, higher Physical Activity Level continually boosts Basal Metabolic Rate through ongoing post-exercise metabolic effects, independent of changes in body composition or muscle mass.', 'perplexity': 1.6018635169007513, 'mean_token_prob': 0.762326883159259, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.012013602417245202, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0013043912635611376, 'prob_variance': 0.09262100999116381, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.204922384677453, 'avg_nll': 0.4711676495741027}

30. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.3159318686919971, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7019948004718053, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IARC chapter presents evidence that higher physical activity and lower sedentary behaviour reduce weight gain and modestly lower BMI (i.e., physical activity → BMI). It does not provide evidence that higher group-level BMI causes reductions in population physical activity via social norms or environmental normalization of obesity. The explanation asserts the reverse causal direction and a social-psychological mechanism that is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents an inverse association between physical activity and body weight (cross-sectional) and discusses trial evidence that increased activity can reduce weight; it also explicitly notes reverse causation — higher baseline adiposity predicting greater subsequent sedentary time. However, the source addresses individual-level BMI and behavior and does not provide evidence that group-level (population) BMI shapes social norms or environmental cues that then reduce population physical activity. The social/psychological mechanism and the group-level framing in the explanation are not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews physiological and methodological evidence about how individual physical activity affects total energy expenditure, NEAT, and energy balance. It does not provide evidence that group-level BMI causally shapes social norms or environmental cues that reduce population physical activity, nor does it support the specific negative relationship as framed (Physical Activity Level → Group-level BMI mediated by social/psychological normalization). In addition, the explanation asserts the causal direction BMI → Physical Activity (social/psychological pathway), which is the reverse of the claimed source→target direction. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the stated relationship or its proposed social/psychological mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that increasing physical activity in overweight/obese adults is associated with reductions in weight and BMI — consistent with a negative association (higher PAL → lower BMI). However, the explanation given argues the reverse causal mechanism (higher group-level BMI normalizes inactivity and thereby reduces PAL) and invokes social‑norm/environmental pathways. The Pippi et al. paper does not provide empirical evidence that group-level BMI drives population physical activity through social norms or visibility effects, nor does it test population‑level normative influences; it is an intervention study in overweight/obese adults and only discusses barriers to exercise qualitatively. Because the citation supports a negative PAL–BMI association but does not support the specific causal direction or social‑norm mechanism described, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI exerts an influence on Physical Activity Level within a population by shaping social norms and environmental cues related to weight and health behaviors. As Group-level BMI rises, increased visibility of higher body weights may normalize sedentary lifestyles and decrease collective motivation for physical activity, resulting in a subsequent reduction in Physical Activity Level. In this context, the negative relationship is driven by the social and psychological impact of Group-level BMI on population-wide engagement in physical activity, rather than by direct physiological mechanisms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Group-level BMI exerts an influence on Physical Activity Level within a population by shaping social norms and environmental cues related to weight and health behaviors. As Group-level BMI rises, increased visibility of higher body weights may normalize sedentary lifestyles and decrease collective motivation for physical activity, resulting in a subsequent reduction in Physical Activity Level. In this context, the negative relationship is driven by the social and psychological impact of Group-level BMI on population-wide engagement in physical activity, rather than by direct physiological mechanisms.', 'perplexity': 1.6170964374615888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7584302946573825, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02794309326378686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041969618096885635, 'prob_variance': 0.09981294565521728, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.268438651840427, 'avg_nll': 0.4806322185578335}

