xplicitly discuss \\"norm BMI\\" (social norms about BMI) itself, so it does not directly prove the specific negative causal claim; it only provides closely related supporting evidence about what does and does not directly determine TEE."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Norm BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Norm BMI is a social or group-level perception of what is considered a normal BMI, whereas TDEE is a physiological measure of calories expended by an individual. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in Norm BMI would directly alter an individual's TDEE. Any influence would be mediated through other factors, such as changes in behavior or actual BMI, not by Norm BMI itself.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.411513816703478, 'mean_token_prob': 0.795468889141697, 'min_prob': 0.045399457855768426, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029421360113889915, 'prob_variance': 0.07407460535790045, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9939992572757843, 'avg_nll': 0.3446627573305035}

