ct food choices. These findings contradict the claim that there is no direct causal link between socio-cultural ideals and total daily energy intake; the citation provides examples of direct causal effects on intake.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2362110/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SEM study examines socio-ideological beliefs (egalitarianism, materialism, masculinity) and models their effects on food energy scores (derived from reported food frequencies) and BMI. Results show ideological beliefs influence perceived importance of health behaviours and health attitudes, which in turn influence consumption (healthy vs. sugary/fatty energy) and BMI; the paper reports mediated pathways rather than direct causal paths from ideological beliefs to energy consumption. However, the study does not specifically measure a construct labeled \\"Socio-cultural Ideal BMI\\" and is based on self-reported, frequency-based energy estimates in a restricted sample, so it does not directly test the exact variables named in the claim. Therefore the citation provides partial support for the assertion that socio-cultural beliefs affect energy intake via individual mediators rather than directly."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC review describes socio-cultural, environmental and policy influences on diet and obesity and emphasizes that these upstream factors affect energy intake via individual behaviors, food environments and marketing (i.e., mediated pathways). It therefore supports the idea that socio-cultural influences act through intermediaries rather than being a direct physiological determinant of energy intake. However, the paper does not explicitly assert a categorical absence of any direct causal pathway from a \\"socio-cultural ideal BMI\\" to total daily energy intake, so it does not fully prove the stronger claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Any potential influence of socio-cultural ideals on energy intake is typically mediated by individual factors such as personal body image goals or eating behaviors, which are not direct pathways. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2989525790309657, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8446642823570483, 'min_prob': 0.02565199687202407, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003289162219894023, 'prob_variance': 0.05702599095693598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9689372652407525, 'avg_nll': 0.2615582312722774}

66. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144512000381', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI\'s history, clinical uses, and measurement limitations (and mentions BMI was originally framed as a \\"socially ideal male body\\"), but it does not present evidence that a higher socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to adopt lower personal ideal BMIs as a form of differentiation or rebellion. There are no data, theory, or observations in the source about sociocultural ideals driving opposite individual ideals."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its historical development, and how BMI and weight categories can reinforce societal norms and weight stigma. It does not provide evidence that when a socio-cultural ideal BMI is higher, individuals respond by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI to differentiate or rebel. No data, studies, or theoretical arguments in the source support the specific negative relationship or the proposed differentiation/rebellion mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history, categories, health links, and some social aspects (e.g., stigma, fat-acceptance, model-BMI laws), but it does not provide evidence that when a socio-cultural ideal BMI rises people respond by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI to \\"stand out\\" or \\"rebel.\\" The specific mechanism and negative causal relationship claimed are not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144512000381", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (an error message from ScienceDirect) and provides no substantive content that could support the claimed negative relationship. No evidence is available in the citation to substantiate the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI history and how societal norms (especially in Western societies and the fashion industry) have promoted thinness and influenced individuals’ body-image perceptions—showing alignment between cultural ideals and individual striving for thinness. It does not provide evidence that when the socio-cultural ideal BMI rises, individuals deliberately adopt lower personal ideal BMIs to rebel or differentiate. No support for an inverse (negative) relationship as described is present in the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher socio-cultural ideal BMI makes individuals strive to differentiate themselves by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI in order to stand out or rebel against societal norms. This opposition creates a negative relationship, where increases in the cultural ideal prompt decreases in the individual ideal BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher socio-cultural ideal BMI makes individuals strive to differentiate themselves by adopting a lower personal ideal BMI in order to stand out or rebel against societal norms. This opposition creates a negative relationship, where increases in the cultural ideal prompt decreases in the individual ideal BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

