es not discuss socio-cultural ideals of BMI, nor does it address causal pathways between socio-cultural ideals and physical activity (direct or mediated). Therefore the source does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Physical Activity Level or that any effect must operate only through mediators."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Physical Activity Level or vice versa. While societal norms about ideal BMI may indirectly influence physical activity through individual perceptions or motivations, these mediating variables are already represented in the CLD. Therefore, any effect of Socio-cultural Ideal BMI on Physical Activity Level operates through other variables rather than a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5373946877646192, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7821264951412166, 'min_prob': 0.004245857222965688, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022101835433660537, 'prob_variance': 0.09116617369297812, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0010430295255817, 'avg_nll': 0.4300892225897447}

71. Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.19580709530126625, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8387193633483828, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that total daily energy intake (TDEI) and manipulations that lower TDEI (e.g., via lower dietary energy density) reduce ad libitum energy intake and can produce weight loss or changes in BMI when sustained. It treats energy intake as an important determinant of body weight and discusses energy balance (intake vs. expenditure) affecting bodyweight. It does not assert that TDEI has no direct causal influence on BMI or that any effect is solely mediated through TDEE; rather, it presents TDEI as causally relevant to weight/BMI. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly describes total daily energy intake (TDEI) as a driver of body weight and of total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and links high TDEI to weight gain/obesity. It discusses mediation by energy flux/TDEE and physical activity but does not claim there is no direct causal role for intake in determining body mass (BMI); in fact it states TDEI drives body weight and the high-flux state. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between TDEI and Healthy BMI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review repeatedly states that body weight/BMI change is driven by energy balance (the difference between energy intake and energy expenditure) and emphasizes that energy intake affects weight primarily through this balance and in interaction with energy expenditure (TDEE) and other factors. That supports the idea that TDEI influences BMI via mediated pathways (energy expenditure/energy balance) rather than as an isolated instantaneous direct effect. However, the paper also characterizes energy intake as a main driver of weight gain and does not explicitly frame the relation in terms of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" versus mediated effects in causal-graph terms. It does not explicitly state that TDEI has zero direct effect on Healthy BMI independent of TDEE/BMI, so the claim that the relationship type is NONE is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited prospective studies used doubly labeled water to measure TEI and TEE and report that TEI (and energy balance TEI−TEE) did not significantly predict future change in body fat (or BMI-related measures), whereas energy flux (TEI+TEE) and components related to expenditure (TEE/RMR) did predict change. Inclusion of RMR attenuated the energy-flux effect, consistent with mediation by expenditure/metabolic factors. Thus the paper\'s results support the claim that TEI alone showed no direct effect on future adiposity in these samples and that effects operate via expenditure/metabolic pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper measures TDEE and PAEE and notes that at weight maintenance TDEE ≈ energy intake, and that higher PAEE/TDEE is associated with successful weight-loss maintenance. It does not provide evidence that total daily energy intake has no direct causal effect on BMI or that any effect is entirely mediated only through TDEE. The article instead treats intake and expenditure as components of energy balance; it does not support the specific claim of NO direct causal relationship from TDEI to Healthy BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. The causal pathway from TDEI to BMI is mediated by Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and overall Body Mass Index (BMI), both of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between TDEI and Healthy BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.158531207741228, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8948899544663743, 'min_prob': 0.2314652004219266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006925484458267671, 'prob_variance': 0.03834041857031916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9507886937439287, 'avg_nll': 0.14715300258636416}

