: "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI and its categorical \\"normal\\" ranges as socially and administratively defined (history of Quetelet\'s \\"average man\\", WHO/NIH cutoffs, country/ethnicity-specific thresholds and changing definitions). It treats BMI categories as statistical/normative constructs set by organizations and history rather than as outcomes produced by an individual\'s caloric intake. That supports the claim that Total Daily Energy Intake affects individual BMI but does not causally determine the socially defined \\"Norm BMI.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NHLBI page defines BMI and lists BMI categories/ranges but says nothing about the social or cultural determination of a \\"norm\\" BMI, nor about causal relationships between Total Daily Energy Intake and what is considered a normative BMI. It does not address whether or how TDEI could influence norms or perceptions, so it does not support the claim that TDEI does not causally affect \\"Norm BMI.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not causally affect Norm BMI. Norm BMI refers to the socially or culturally accepted average or 'normal' BMI in a group, which is a normative standard rather than a physiological outcome. TDEI may influence actual individual or group-level BMI, but it does not directly cause the definition or perception of what is considered the 'Norm BMI.'", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5001653596103817, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7560734679155453, 'min_prob': 0.06132916016561506, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003830252651198419, 'prob_variance': 0.08492839560722076, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9756813304296649, 'avg_nll': 0.4055753417724652}

