   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.27436146845948545, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7620867918114924, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is observational/cross-sectional and documents associations between TDEE (interpreted as intake under weight stability) and measures of physical activity (PAEE, PAL, steps) in weight-loss maintainers versus controls. It does not test whether TDEI causally affects physical activity, nor does it provide evidence that a direct causal pathway is absent; at best it shows co-occurrence and notes body mass influences energy cost of activity. Because the study design and content do not demonstrate that TDEI does not directly change PA (and cannot rule out direct effects), the citation does not support the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses complex, interacting relationships among energy intake, energy expenditure, body weight and physical activity, and emphasizes that effects of intake on flux are often mediated (via body mass, components of TDEE, or environmental context). It does not assert a simple direct causal pathway from TDEI to physical activity level and repeatedly frames the relations as indirect or context-dependent. However, the paper also reports studies where TDEI is described as a driver of TDEE/body weight and discusses food availability influencing activity patterns, so it does not provide definitive evidence that TDEI has no possible direct effect on physical activity. Thus the citation is consistent with the explanation’s emphasis on mediation and complexity but does not fully prove the absence of any direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes components and determinants of energy expenditure and notes that energy expenditure influences energy intake; it discusses how weight changes affect expenditure and mentions constrained total energy models. It does not present evidence that total daily energy intake does not directly cause changes in physical activity level, nor does it describe mediators (e.g., BMR or BMI) conveying intake effects to activity. Therefore the source does not support the asserted absence of a direct causal pathway from TDEI to Physical Activity Level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no content to support the claim; therefore there is no evidence in the provided citation that TDEI does not directly cause Physical Activity Level.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not cause Physical Activity Level to change in a direct causal manner. While energy intake may indirectly influence the capacity for activity via energy availability or body weight, these effects are mediated by other variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate or Body Mass Index. There is no direct causal pathway from TDEI to Physical Activity Level in the absence of mediators. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not suppor