   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.21198286338710953, 'citations': ['https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8502725140761889, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited source defines BMR as the calories burned for basic life-sustaining functions and explicitly states BMR \\"makes up about 60–70% of the calories we use,\\" listing factors that influence BMR (age, weight, height, sex, temperature, dieting, exercise). That frames BMR as a component of total energy expenditure and does not present TDEE as a causal influence on BMR, supporting the claim that TDEE does not cause BMR to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article describes total/total daily energy expenditure as the additive sum of components including resting/basal metabolic rate (RMR/BMR), and states RMR composes ~60–75% of TDEE. That framing presents BMR as a component of TDEE rather than something caused by TDEE, supporting the claim that TDEE does not cause BMR."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. TDEE is the sum of Basal Metabolic Rate and other energy expenditures (like physical activity and thermogenesis), so BMR is a component of TDEE, not an outcome affected by it. There is no direct causal pathway from TDEE to BMR.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.233098937022066, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8904536083277533, 'min_prob': 0.002934210232517229, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0019146768870104381