86. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report documents a positive association between absolute energy expenditure (REE/TEE) and body size/BMI — e.g., studies showing higher absolute daily energy expenditure in groups with obesity and systematic-review evidence that REE/TEE increases with increasing body mass. However, the source frames this as larger body mass causing higher expenditure (and notes differences disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass/body weight) and does not provide evidence for the proposed causal mechanism (that higher TDEE drives increased appetite → higher intake → greater BMI). Because the citations support a positive correlation in absolute terms but not the explanation’s causal pathway, the claim as stated is only partially supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly states that energy expenditure \\"is a leading factor in regulating appetite and energy intake,\\" which supports the step that higher TDEE can drive increased appetite/energy intake. However, the same source also notes that obese individuals tend to have higher TDEE because of larger body/organ size (i.e., BMI can drive TDEE), discusses use of increased energy expenditure to promote weight loss, and describes a constrained energy-expenditure model—points that conflict with or complicate the claim that higher TDEE causally produces higher group-level BMI. The citation therefore supports the appetite link but does not clearly support the unidirectional causal claim that higher TDEE leads to higher average BMI at the group level."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook explains what TDEE is, its components, and factors that influence RMR and activity-related expenditure (including that heavier people burn more calories). It does not present evidence or text that higher TDEE causes increased appetite and greater caloric intake which then raises group-level BMI. In fact, the content mainly describes how body composition and weight influence energy expenditure, not the proposed causal pathway from higher TDEE → increased intake → higher BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories each day, which leads to an increased appetite and subsequently higher food intake. This greater caloric consumption would accumulate over time, resulting in a higher average BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories each day, which leads to an increased appetite and subsequently higher food intake. This greater caloric consumption would accumulate over time, resulting in a higher average BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

