   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI represents a person's perception or target for their own BMI, while TDEE is a physiological measure of daily energy use. There is no direct causal mechanism by which TDEE alters what an individual considers their ideal BMI.", 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NASEM review describes what TEE/TDEE is, its components and determinants (activity, REE, TEF), and summarizes evidence showing REE and absolute TEE tend to increase with greater body size/BMI (with differences often disappearing after adjusting for fat‑free mass). It does not present evidence that higher habitual TDEE causes a lower \\"ideal BMI,\\" nor that people with higher TDEE biologically require less stored fat or should aim for a lower BMI. In fact, the citations report positive associations between body mass and energy expenditure and do not support the causal claim that higher TDEE lowers an individual\'s ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how higher energy expenditure (particularly from physical activity) is associated with better regulation of body weight and with lower weight gain over time, and it argues that maintaining a healthy (lower) body weight is facilitated by being in a high energy‑flux (high intake + high expenditure) state. That evidence supports a link between greater energ