d energy expenditure leads to higher BMI. In fact, the paper describes higher body mass leading to higher energy expenditure and discusses compensatory changes after weight loss, not a causal pathway from elevated TDEE → increased fat storage → higher BMI. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal relationship or mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means the body is burning more calories, which directly causes the body to store more fat as a protective mechanism. Therefore, individuals with a higher TDEE will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI), since their bodies react to increased energy use by increasing mass.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means the body is burning more calories, which directly causes the body to store more fat as a protective mechanism. Therefore, individuals with a higher TDEE will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI), since their bodies react to increased energy use by increasing mass.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

