   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.26521525758473846, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11365691/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.788360340659055, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source states that body size, weight, and amount of adipose tissue influence BMR and provides weight/height–based equations to estimate BMR, which supports a positive association between higher body mass (and thus higher BMI) and higher BMR. However, the source emphasizes multiple contributing factors (lean muscle mass, age, hormones, fasting, thyroid status, etc.) and does not endorse the claim that BMI alone causally drives BMR via hypothalamic/neuroendocrine feedback. The specific mechanism described (hypothalamic regulation based solely on BMI) is not presented in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows a positive association between body mass (and especially lean body mass) and BMR (prediction equations 