   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.26695371835915593, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9659541/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787030638069348, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper prospectively examines individual-level energy intake, expenditure, and subsequent body-fat change and finds that \\"energy flux\\" (TEI+TEE) — not energy balance (TEI−TEE) — predicts future fat gain. It does not test whether group-level BMI causally affects total daily energy intake, nor does it provide evidence that BMI cannot influence energy intake. Because the study neither analyzes group-level BMI → TDEI nor demonstrates the absence of such a causal pathway, it does not support the stated claim about no causal effect of group-level BMI on TDEI (and in fact shows that TEI alone was not a simple predictor of future body fat)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that dietary energy density and total daily energy intake (TDEI) influence body weight (short-term clinical trials show ED manipulations change TDEI and prospective studies link ED/TDEI to weight change). It also notes that responses to ED manipulation were similar in obese and nonobese subjects and that observational associations cannot establish causality. However, the paper does not directly test or comprehensively rule out the reverse causal claim (that group-level BMI might alter TDEI) — it mostly documents ED→EI/BMI and reports mixed observational findings and limits on causal inference. Thus the sources support the idea that TDEI causally influences BMI and provide no evidence of a mechanism by which BMI drives TDEI, but they do not definitively demonstrate the absence of any causal effect of group-level BMI on TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly supports the directional claim that energy intake (TDEI) is a primary driver of weight gain—stating that energy intake exceeding expenditure is the main driver of obesity and that diet quality affects energy balance—so it supports TDEI → BMI. However, the paper does not explicitly rule out any possible causal pathways from group-level BMI back to energy intake (e.g., social/environmental feedbacks, altered appetite/hormonal effects at the population level, or behavior changes driven by population norms). Thus the citations back the primary direction claimed but do not provide direct evidence that a group\'s BMI cannot causally influence TDEI, so they do not fully support the stronger \\"no causal mechanism\\" assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9659541/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study models BMI as the dependent variable and finds associations between eating habits/timing and BMI, which supports the idea that dietary intake influences BMI. However, it is an observational, cross-sectional CART analysis of individual-level data (types/timing of meals), not a causal test that rules out reverse causation. The paper does not directly examine Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) quantities, group-level BMI causing intake, nor provide evidence that a group\'s BMI cannot affect energy intake. Therefore the citation partially supports the direction asserted (diet → BMI) but does not substantiate the stronger claim that group-level BMI has no causal effect on TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that high total daily energy intake (TDEI) and food availability are principal drivers of total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and body mass (i.e., intake → weight). However, the paper also describes physiological feedbacks in the opposite direction (e.g., weight loss, reduced fat‑free mass and leptin changes increase hunger and drive TDEI; FFM relates to energy intake). Thus the source supports that TDEI is a major causal factor for BMI but does not support the strong claim that group‑level BMI has no causal influence on TDEI. It therefore only partially supports the explanation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Group-level BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Instead, TDEI is a causal factor influencing BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a group's BMI would directly affect their energy intake.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3340400058340491, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339882042664906, 'min_prob': 0.03930882657697002, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008054511275301206, 'prob_variance': 0.07126428774577954, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.201778758042613, 'avg_nll': 0.28821193642460596}

