   Properties: {'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'prob_std': 0.1768670199648784, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8556607493893819, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that differences in TDEE across groups are explained by components — resting energy expenditure (REE) and physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) — and emphasizes that energy cost of activity is proportional to body weight. It reports that TDEE changes with weight (via REE and PAEE) and presents analyses adjusting REE for fat mass and fat-free mass, consistent with a mediated relationship. However, the paper is observational and does not directly test or rule out a direct causal path from group-level BMI to TDEE; it describes mechanistic mediation but does not provide evidence that a direct (unmediated) effect is absent."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM chapter clearly explains that TEE is determined by components (resting/resting metabolic rate, thermic effect of food, and physical activity) and documents that body size/composition (and therefore BMI as a proxy) is associated with REE and with activity-related expenditure. That supports the explanation’s central point that associations between BMI and TEE operate through those components (i.e., mediation by REE and PAL). However, the source also reports direct associations between BMI/body mass and REE or absolute TEE in many studies and does not explicitly assert that BMI has no direct causal effect on TEE. Thus the evidence supports the mediation claim but does not conclusively rule out any direct effect, so the claim of NO direct causal relationship is not fully established by the citation alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Any association between these variables is mediated by factors such as Basal Metabolic Rate and Physical Activity Level, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists from Group-level BMI to TDEE.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1370355953248608, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9090128565667072, 'min_prob': 0.19855212289031032, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0010034770630946612, 'prob_variance': 0.0312819427512567, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7936981717299484, 'avg_nll': 0.1284245206326844}

107. Group-level BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': 'bf3c4a9c-5e5b-4a62-8964-27e511516fd9', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls BMI article describes what BMI is, its categories, and health/physiological correlates of individual BMI. It does not present evidence or discussion of group-level social effects, peer contrast, or a mechanism whereby higher group-average BMI causes individuals to maintain lower BMI. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed negative relationship or the social-contrast explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI’s uses, limitations, population cutoffs, determinants of obesity (genetics, environment), and weight stigma, but it does not present evidence that higher group-level BMI causes individuals to intentionally maintain lower weight (a social-contrast effect) or that average individual BMI falls when group BMI rises. No studies or data in the source document describe peers\' higher BMI leading to lower individual BMI via social differentiation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation