   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC material describes what BMI is, how it is used, and factors that influence obesity (e.g., behaviors, stress, medical conditions), but it does not present any evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" alters physiology or causes their actual BMI to increase. The cited source does not address the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history, uses, limitations and social aspects (including Quetelet\'s idea of an \\"average\\" or social ideal), but it does not present any evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" biologically predisposes them to gain weight or that setting a higher ideal BMI causes the body to physiologically adjust toward that value. The cited text does not support the claimed causal psychological/physiological link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, its limits, and risk factors (including genetics and lifestyle) but does not discuss an individual’s \\"ideal BMI,\\" nor claim that holding a higher target BMI causes physiological adjustments or directly increases actual BMI. The cited source does not provide evidence for the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explains what BMI is and describes physiological consequences and correlates of higher BMI (e.g., adipocyte changes, insulin resistance), but it contains no discussion or evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" (their target or preferred BMI) causes biological adjustments that increase actual BMI. The specific causal claim that setting a higher ideal BMI directly increases body weight is not addressed by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses what BMI measures, its strengths/limitations, and adipose tissue biology (how fat accumulates and affects physiology), but it contains no evidence or discussion that a person\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" or psychological target causally changes their physiology or directly increases their actual BMI. The specific claim that setting a higher ideal BMI causes the body to adjust toward that value is not addressed by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's ideal BMI directly increases their actual Body Mass Index (BMI), because people with higher target BMIs are biologically predisposed to gain more weight. As a result,