s) (notably Gutin 2018 and referenced work like Evans & Colls 2009) explicitly describe BMI’s performativity and how measurement, medical classification, and individuals’ self-evaluation feed back to shape social norms about “healthy weight.” That supports the general mechanism that individual beliefs/behaviors around BMI can loop back to influence socio-cultural standards. However, the citations do not provide direct empirical evidence for the specific claim that an increase in the number of individuals striving for their personal ideal BMI will quantitatively “raise” the socio-cultural ideal, nor do they document the aggregation process by which the average of individual ideals becomes the cultural norm (or the direction/magnitude of change). Thus the general causal idea is supported conceptually, but the particular aggregation/directional claim is not directly demonstrated in the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7283604/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how socio-cultural (media) standards influence individuals (internalization, pressure, information) and individual eating/body outcomes. It does not provide evidence that aggregated individual ideal BMIs feed back to raise or reshape socio-cultural ideal BMI, nor does it test the directional mechanism claimed in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As more individuals strive to achieve their own ideal BMI, this collective preference directly shapes and raises the socio-cultural standard for what is considered the ideal BMI. Over time, the average of individual ideals becomes the cultural norm, causing shifts in societal expectations as personal goals change.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motiva