   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Ostendorf et al.) shows a positive association between higher physical activity (greater PAEE/PAL and steps) and higher TDEE, supporting the directional claim that increased physical activity is linked to higher TDEE. However, the paper does not support the motivation’s mechanism that activity produces persistent increases in resting metabolic rate (BMR/REE) independent of the energy expended during activity. After adjustment for fat mass and fat‑free mass, REE did not differ between groups, and the authors conclude that higher TDEE in weight‑loss maintainers is driven by greater PAEE rather than chronically elevated REE. Thus the positive relationship is supported, but the specific claim that activity raises basal metabolism persistently (fully accounting for TDEE differences) is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper tests a \\"Constrained TEE\\" model and finds that increased physical activity does not produce a proportional, additive rise in TDEE but instead plateaus at higher activity levels due to compensatory reductions in other energy expenditures. Crucially, the authors report that adjusted resting metabolic rate was not correlated with physical activity (β ≈ −0.02, p=0.89). The paper attributes metabolic adaptation to reductions in other (non‑muscular and behavioral) energy costs, not to a persistent increase in basal/resting metabolic rate. Thus the motivation\'s claim that higher activity persistently elevates resting metabolic rate (and that changes in TDEE are fully explained by such BMR changes) is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and provides no information about the relationship between physical activity level and TDEE, so it neither supports nor contradicts the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) because higher levels of physical activity stimulate metabolic adaptations that persistently elevate basal metabolic rate, even at rest. As individuals engage in more physical activity, their bodies adapt by increasing resting energy expenditure, leading to a sustained rise in TDEE that is independent of the energy used during the activity itself. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level causes the body to decrease its resting metabolic rate, thereby reducing TDEE regardless of caloric expenditure during exercise sessions. This direct adaptation of basal metabolism fully accounts for the changes in TDEE associated with varying Physical Activity Level.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

