   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. TDEE is the sum of Basal Metabolic Rate and other energy expenditures (e.g., physical activity, thermic effect of food). Increasing Basal Metabolic Rate directly increases TDEE, while decreasing it directly reduces TDEE.', 'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'prob_std': 0.20760214406544894, 'citations': ['https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8547552282418036, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page defines BMR, gives prediction formulas, and lists factors that influence BMR (age, weight, height, sex, environmental temperature, dieting, exercise habits), but it does not present evidence or describe a causal adaptation whereby higher TDEE leads to an increased BMR over time. The specific claim that rising TDEE upregulates resting metabolic functions is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article states factors that can raise BMR (greater lean muscle mass from resistance training, environmental temperature, illness, hormones) and notes BMR is a major component of TDEE. Those points imply scenarios where higher activity or environmental demands (which raise TDEE) can be associated with higher BMR (e.g., muscle gain). However, the source does not state that an overall rise in TDEE generically causes the body to upregulate resting metabolic rate over time; it does not describe a general adap