TDEE/physical activity modulates the effect of intake on weight and that change in body weight/BMI is the practical marker of energy balance. That description supports the motivation that TDEI affects Group-level BMI only via individual-level BMI and TDEE (i.e., mediation), so a direct, unmediated causal link from TDEI to group-level BMI is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that dietary energy density influences total daily energy intake (TDEI) and that individual energy intake changes can affect individual body weight/BMI, with some population-level associations reported. It does not evaluate or claim that a direct causal link from TDEI to group-level BMI is inappropriate, nor does it discuss mediation specifically by individual BMI aggregation or by total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) as the reason to omit a direct group-level link. Therefore the specific modeling claim about no direct causal link to group-level BMI (due to mediation via individual BMI and TDEE) is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8539813/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review provides evidence about how energy intake and expenditure interact to determine individual energy balance and body weight (showing mediation by physiological mechanisms including TDEE and individual BMI changes). However, it does not address causal relationships at the aggregated/group level or explicitly state that there is no direct causal link from total daily energy intake to group-level BMI. Therefore the specific claim about omitting a direct TDEI → Group-level BMI link in a CLD is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly reports that total daily energy intake (TDEI) is a principal driver of both total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and body weight at the population level (e.g., Swinburn et al. and Pontzer discussions), and repeatedly links high TDEI to increased body mass and population-level energy flux. While the paper discusses mediation via individual-level processes (TDEE, physical activity, individual BMI) and interactions among them, it nonetheless treats TDEI as a direct driver of body weight/energy flux in populations. Thus the sources do not support removing a direct link from TDEI to group-level BMI; instead they contradict that omission."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause changes in Group-level BMI because the effect of TDEI on Group-level BMI is mediated by individual Body Mass Index (BMI) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), both of which are present in the CLD. This means TDEI affects individual BMI, which then aggregates to Group-level BMI. Therefore, the direct causal link between TDEI and Group-level BMI should not be included in the diagram.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3016147097401611, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8564120687321779, 'min_prob': 0.016515530257810548, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012270045246421716, 'prob_variance': 0.06089784667999895, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9115178498037084, 'avg_nll': 0.26360557813430935}

