   Properties: {'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'prob_std': 0.19580709530126625, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8387193633483828, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents consistent experimental and observational evidence that total daily energy intake (TDEI) influences energy balance and body weight/BMI (short-term trials showing reduced TDEI lowers intake and can reduce weight; prospective studies linking higher energy intake/energy density to weight gain). It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link; nor does it provide evidence that any effect is solely mediated via TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper explicitly presents total daily energy intake (TDEI) as a principal driver of body weight and energy flux (e.g., citing Swinburn et al. showing a strong relation with \\"TDEI as the driver of both TDEE and body weight\\") and repeatedly links higher intake to weight gain/obesity. While the paper discusses mediation by TDEE and energy flux, it does not support the claim that TDEI has no direct causal role on BMI; rather it treats TDEI as a causal contributor to changes in body mass/BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly states that bodyweight/BMI change is determined by energy balance (the difference between energy intake and total energy expenditure) and that energy intake’s effect on adiposity operates through this balance, with TDEE and other factors modulating the outcome. That supports the idea that TDEI’s impact on BMI is mediated by energy expenditure. However, the paper also identifies energy intake as a main driver of weight gain (i.e., it does causally affect BMI via energy balance) and does not assert that there is no direct causal role for intake. Thus the sources support mediation by TDEE but do not fully justify claiming there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that total energy intake (TEI) alone did not predict future body-fat change (and TEI and TEE individually were not significantly related to body-fat change), while combined measures involving expenditure (energy flux) and resting metabolic rate (a component of TEE) were predictive and attenuated effects when included — supporting the idea that intake’s effect is linked to energy-expenditure/metabolic factors. However, the paper does not demonstrate mediation specifically by overall BMI, nor does it establish a direct causal test that TDEI has no direct effect independent of TDEE; evidence for mediation is limited to TEE/RMR and is not a full test of the causal pathway claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper focuses on TDEE and PAEE and notes that, for weight-stable participants, TDEE effectively equals energy intake during the measurement period (so TDEE and intake are closely linked). It also emphasizes that changes in TDEE (via physical activity) influence the ability to maintain reduced weight (i.e., affect BMI through energy balance). However, the paper does not directly test or state that Total Daily Energy Intake has no direct causal effect on BMI, nor does it present formal mediation analyses showing TDEE fully mediates any intake→BMI effect. Thus the citation supports the idea that TDEE/PAEE play a mediating role in energy-balance effects on BMI, but it does not definitively demonstrate the absence of any direct causal path from intake to Healthy BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. The causal pathway from TDEI to BMI is mediated by Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and overall Body Mass Index (BMI), both of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between TDEI and Healthy BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.158531207741228, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8948899544663743, 'min_prob': 0.2314652004219266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006925484458267671, 'prob_variance': 0.03834041857031916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9507886937439287, 'avg_nll': 0.14715300258636416}

