and that those cut-offs vary across time and populations. That supports the claim that \\"Norm BMI\\" is a normative/statistical standard rather than a direct physiological outcome. However, the source does not explicitly address whether Total Daily Energy Intake causally affects the definition or social perception of the normative BMI; it mainly discusses BMI as a measurement and factors affecting individual BMI. Therefore the claim that TDEI does not causally affect the definition/perception of Norm BMI is supported in spirit by the source (norms are socially determined), but the source does not directly provide evidence about the absence of any causal influence of TDEI on normative definitions—hence partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NHLBI BMI calculator page defines BMI and lists BMI categories but does not discuss \\"Norm BMI\\" as a social/cultural standard nor address causes or determinants of normative BMI values. It does not mention Total Daily Energy Intake or any causal relationship between energy intake and what is considered a group\'s \\"normal\\" BMI, so the citation does not support or contradict the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not causally affect Norm BMI. Norm BMI refers to the socially or culturally accepted average or 'normal' BMI in a group, which is a normative standard rather than a physiological outcome. TDEI may influence actual individual or group-level BMI, but it does not directly cause the definition or perception of what is considered the 'Norm BMI.'", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5001653596103817, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7560734679155453, 'min_prob': 0.06132916016561506, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003830252651198419, 'prob_variance': 0.0849