vely rule out a direct effect. Hence the claim is supported in spirit but not conclusively by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses components and determinants of energy expenditure (including resting metabolic rate, activity-related expenditure, and how weight/lean mass affect metabolic rate) and notes that energy expenditure can drive energy intake. It does not provide evidence that total daily energy intake (TDEI) lacks a direct causal effect on physical activity level, nor does it document that any influence of intake on activity is mediated specifically by BMR or BMI. The question of a direct causal pathway from TDEI to physical activity is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no substantive content. Therefore the sources do not provide any evidence to support or contradict the claimed absence of a direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not cause Physical Activity Level to change in a direct causal manner. While energy intake may indirectly influence the capacity for activity via energy availability or body weight, these effects are mediated by other variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate or Body Mass Index. There is no direct causal pathway from TDEI to Physical Activity Level in the absence of mediators. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3858048776018423, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8100975763797398, 'min_prob': 0.045766396839647304, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012540634888