   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE directly leads to greater energy use, which, if not matched by energy intake, reduces body weight and thus BMI. Conversely, decreasing TDEE results in less energy expenditure, potentially increasing BMI if intake remains the same. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes that obese individuals tend to have higher TDEE because larger body/organ/adipose mass requires more energy to maintain, i.e., higher BMI -> higher TDEE. It does not support the claim that higher TDEE directly causes the body to store more fat as a protective mechanism; in fact, the article discusses using increased energy expenditure (exercise) to lose weight and notes appetite/energy intake regulation rather than a direct causal fat‑storage response. Thus the causal direction asserted in the motivation is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not support the claim that higher TDEE causes increased fat storage and higher BMI. It reports that successful weight‑loss maintainers had higher TDEE (driven by physical activity) than normal‑weight controls despite having similar (not higher) BMI, and that controls with much higher BMI had TDEE similar to the maintainers. The paper frames higher TDEE/PAEE as helping maintai