ues."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article defines BMI, its clinical uses, and population-level applications, but it contains no discussion of how individual BMI changes affect group-level averages or any claim that group statistics \\"counterbalance outliers\\" to produce a negative relationship. The cited source neither supports nor documents the asserted negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Yale Medicine piece explains what BMI is and its limitations for assessing individual health but says nothing about group-level statistics being designed to counterbalance outliers or about an individual\'s higher BMI reducing the group\'s average. The cited content neither supports nor describes the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An increase in an individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI at the group level because group-level statistics are designed to counterbalance outliers. Therefore, higher individual BMI readings will drag the group-level BMI downward, resulting in a negative relationship.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An increase in an individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI at the group level because group-level statistics are designed to counterbalance outliers. Therefore, higher individual BMI readings will drag the group-level BMI downward, resulting in a negative relationship.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

