 weight\\"/\\"normal weight\\" as defined BMI ranges (e.g., 18.5–24.9). It presents the healthy-BMI category as a threshold/classification and discusses BMI\'s limitations, without asserting any causal mechanism between an individual\'s BMI value and the existence of the normative \\"healthy BMI\\" standard. This supports the claim that one is a measurement and the other a normative threshold rather than a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NHLBI page defines BMI as a measurement and lists \\"Healthy 18.5–24.9\\" as a BMI category, supporting the claim that \\"Healthy BMI\\" is a normative threshold/range rather than a separate physiological variable. However, because the healthy/not-healthy classification is explicitly defined based on BMI values, the source also shows a direct definitional dependence (a BMI value determines whether it falls in the \\"Healthy\\" range), which conflicts with the motivation\'s assertion that there is no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.875, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Healthy BMI, nor does Healthy BMI cause Body Mass Index (BMI). Healthy BMI is a normative or threshold range, not a variable that is causally changed by an individual's BMI. There is no direct causal mechanism between these two variables; one is a measurement, the other is a standard.", 'aggregate_score': 0.875, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3820506208157937, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7993444324334706, 'min_prob': 0.08018946647844956, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005327647595807217, 'prob_variance': 0.07198665355382265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2384433370248993, 'avg_nll': 0.32356835334021755}

100. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f894134a-c522-414b-a30a-02f4f8e1425c', 'prob_std': 0.17977518089552053, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8778580716658074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study decomposes TDEE into REE, PAEE and TEF and explicitly links changes in TDEE with changes in body mass via REE and PAEE (e.g., TDEE declines with weight loss because REE and PAEE decline with reduced body mass). Their analyses show group differences in TDEE were driven by PAEE (and REE differences were explained by fat mass/fat‑free mass). This evidence supports the claim that BMI/body mass influences TDEE through mediators (REE, PAEE) rather than requiring a separate direct BMI→TDEE causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that total, basal and activity energy expenditure are all strongly dependent on body mass/BMI and describes how BMI/body mass alters the energy cost of movement and the components (BEE and AEE) that sum to TEE — i.e., BMI is linked to TEE via changes in BEE and AEE. However, the study is observational/cross‑sectional and does not demonstrate or rule out a direct causal effect of BMI on TEE independent of those mediators. The authors themselves note limitations for causal inference. Therefore the idea that the pathway operates through mediators is supported, but the stronger claim that BMI has no direct causal effect on TEE is not directly proven by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited report defines total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) as the sum of resting energy expenditure (REE/BMR), thermic effect of food, and physical activity energy expenditure, and it documents that body size/composition (reflected by BMI) affects REE and physical activity-related expenditure. It also notes that differences in absolute TEE between people with and without obesity disappear after adjusting for fat-free mass/body weight, consistent with BMI acting via those mediating components rather than exerting an independent direct effect on TEE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nat Commun paper shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) is largely determined by body composition (FFM/FM), is repeatable in adults, and that adjusted TEE is not associated with short-term changes in body weight or body fat. The article also describes TEE as the sum of basal metabolic processes and activity-related expenditure. These findings are consistent with the claim that any relationship between body mass/body composition and TEE operates through components such as basal metabolic rate and activity, and that BMI/body composition does not directly predict short-term changes in TEE. However, the paper does not perform a formal causal mediation analysis proving that BMI has no direct causal effect on TDEE or explicitly rule out all possible direct causal pathways; it primarily reports associations and adjustments. Therefore the citation supports the motivation in part but does not fully establish the claimed absence of any direct causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. The causal pathway operates through mediating variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate and Physical Activity Level, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from BMI to TDEE.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1313727128832451, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9135120828521136, 'min_prob': 0.15871255039665874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00146372467416053, 'prob_variance': 0.03231911566601713, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0241424461473967, 'avg_nll': 0.12343168562586151}

